<section id="about">
<h2>1. Introduction to Engineering Graphics</h2>
<p>Engineering Graphics is the visual language of engineers. It is used to represent objects, structures and designs through technical drawings.</p>
<p>It helps engineers communicate their ideas accurately using lines, symbols, dimensions and different projection methods.</p>
<h3>Objectives</h3>
<ul>
<li>Develop visualisation skills.</li>
<li>Understand technical drawings.</li>
<li>Learn different projection methods.</li>
<li>Improve drawing accuracy and creativity.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section id="tools">
<h2>2. Drawing Instruments</h2>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Drawing Board</h3><p>Provides a flat surface for technical drawing.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>T-Square</h3><p>Used to draw horizontal parallel lines.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Set Squares</h3><p>Used to draw standard angles such as 30°, 45° and 60°.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Compass</h3><p>Used to draw circles and arcs.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Divider</h3><p>Used to measure and transfer distances.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Scale</h3><p>Used to measure and represent dimensions.</p></div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="types">
<h2>3. Types of Engineering Projections</h2>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card">
<h3>Orthographic Projection</h3>
<p>Represents a 3D object through separate 2D views such as front, top and side views.</p>
</div>
<div class="card">
<h3>Isometric Projection</h3>
<p>Represents a three-dimensional object in a single pictorial view.</p>
</div>
<div class="card">
<h3>Perspective Projection</h3>
<p>Shows objects as they appear to the human eye, creating a realistic appearance.</p>
</div>
</div>
<h3>Orthographic vs Isometric</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Orthographic</th><th>Isometric</th></tr>
<tr><td>Uses multiple 2D views.</td><td>Uses one 3D pictorial view.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shows detailed dimensions.</td><td>Helps visualise the overall shape.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Used in manufacturing drawings.</td><td>Used for understanding 3D objects.</td></tr>
</table>
</section>

